- op: discuss  # 863e
  at: '2026-11-17T15:59:04Z'  # 863e
  by: agent  # 863e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 863e
  clock: 5  # 863e
  nonce: 863e  # 863e
  message: "MEASURED BEFORE BUILDING, 2026-11-17 (~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/fast_smart_test_build_11172026/stmt/replay2.py, replay2_out.txt; summary in STATUS.md). (1) THE PLAN'S EVIDENCE WAS HINDSIGHT: replay.py read 'tests that ran a changed statement' from records of the block AFTER the change (it required the index digest to equal the PR's head). Before a merge only the records of the block as it WAS exist. (2) A RULE THAT USES ONLY THOSE: edited/deleted statement -> tests that ran it; INSERTED statement -> tests that ran its anchor (the statement before it in the same list, else the one it is nested in, else the block's entry = every test that reached the block; an except clause anchors to its try; a docstring, global/nonlocal or bare annotation cannot be an anchor); digest moved with no statement changed (signature, decorator) -> every test that reached the block; a NEW function -> nothing of its own (the changed callers' tests and the change's edited test files). (3) RESULT over 45 PRs: on the 989 replayable changed blocks that existed before, that rule holds every test the hindsight rule holds (989 of 989). Cost 6,722 test-seconds against hindsight's 6,830 and block level's 10,957. Of 31 PRs with at least half their blocks replayable, 20 fit 60 test-seconds under both rules. New functions: 106 in 13 fully replayable PRs, 587 tests ran one, all held by the rule or by an edited test file. 1,047 of 2,283 changed blocks were NOT replayable (edited again since). NOT IN THE DATA: a new method overriding an inherited one, reached by unchanged callers (INV-bifih's shape); the selector needs a stated rule for it. (4) WHAT THE DESCRIPTION DID NOT SEE: selection_index drops a block's statement records when a fold stores another digest for it, so after the first local run on an edited function the records a pre-merge selector needs are gone and it would select at block level for exactly the blocks a change edits. The selector therefore needs the index to KEEP superseded records by digest and to CARRY a record FORWARD for tests the change provably does not affect. Both are index-format changes; they are part of this item's work, not optional."  # 863e
- op: discuss  # 7a29
  at: '2026-11-17T17:31:39Z'  # 7a29
  by: agent  # 7a29
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7a29
  clock: 6  # 7a29
  nonce: 7a29  # 7a29
  message: "PR #2232 merged (dev bc46260a7): FIRST PART, RECORDING ONLY. statement_diff.py (which old statements a change makes a test answer for), statement_select.py (the selector), and a `by_statement` object in every row of run_records.jsonl, computed by `coverage-select run-record` BEFORE the run is folded into the index: answers per changed function, tests it would select, by_block, unknown_blocks, failed_reaching_a_changed_block, failed_not_selected. The description's close condition for THIS part holds: a real run wrote a row (several did, 2026-11-17 16:26 UTC on); each rule has a test and a mutation (31 mutations, all red); a kind of changed file the selector has no rule for is reported, not skipped, only in this sense: non-.py changes are simply not in by_statement, and that is NOT YET SAID IN THE ROW. Still owed. THE ITEM STAYS OPEN. Owed before the selector is worth switching on: (1) the index keeps superseded statement records by digest (today an edited function reads 'no record of the block as it was' after its first test run, so every test that reached it is kept); (2) a record is carried forward for tests a change does not affect; (3) rows for the 362 functions the index has never seen (a full local fold); (4) the row names the changed files the selector has no rule for; (5) the new-method rule is a guess. Also merged with it: python.yaml completeness for difflib.SequenceMatcher (both claims gates were red without it), and a fix to my own execution test, which had switched coverage off in CI (its own item)."  # 7a29
